# IRL.OS — COMPLETE FEATURE & INTERACTION SPECIFICATION

## 1. THE CORE RULE

Build IRL.OS as a fully functional personal operating system for real-life progress, not a collection of mockup screens.

Every feature must have a clear purpose, useful content, working interactions, sensible empty states, and persistent data. Do not create buttons that do nothing, fake charts, decorative controls, hardcoded AI answers, or windows that only look interactive.

Keep the existing IRL.OS identity, blue-wave background, premium dark aesthetic, light/dark themes, and floating desktop-window concept. Extend the existing application rather than replacing it with an unrelated design.

The interface should feel like a polished desktop environment combined with a personal productivity application. It must be calm, intuitive, slightly futuristic, and genuinely useful.

Use natural human language throughout the entire application. Avoid robotic AI-generated copy, corporate jargon, technical system labels, excessive cards, unnecessary statistics, and overcomplicated dashboards.

---

# 2. DESKTOP LAYOUT AND BOTTOM NAVIGATION

## The desktop

The desktop is the main application, not a separate demo page.

- Keep the animated blue-wave background visible behind the windows.
- Use elegant floating windows with rounded corners, subtle borders, restrained transparency, and soft shadows.
- Windows must remain readable against the background.
- Allow users to drag windows by their title bars.
- Support minimizing, maximizing, restoring, closing, and reopening windows.
- Prevent windows from becoming inaccessible off-screen.
- Allow multiple windows to remain open simultaneously.
- Give the active window a subtle highlight.
- Keep window transitions smooth and fast.
- Remember useful window positions and sizes during the session.
- Do not force every feature into one giant dashboard.
- Avoid excessive nested cards and unnecessary scrollbars.

Each window must have a clear title, a short human explanation where useful, and the controls relevant to that feature.

## Bottom dock: permanent navigation

Place the main navigation at the bottom of the screen, following the provided reference image.

The dock must contain these nine options in this exact order:

1. Home
2. Today
3. Goals
4. Planner
5. Focus
6. Insights
7. Coach
8. Profile
9. Settings

Use clean, recognizable icons with short labels. Keep the dock centered, compact, and elegant. It should float above the blue-wave background rather than look like a generic website navigation bar.

### Hover and interaction behavior

Every dock item must respond when the user moves their cursor over it.

- Slightly enlarge the hovered icon.
- Add a soft glow or colored highlight.
- Move the icon upward by a few pixels.
- Smoothly animate the label into view or brighten it.
- Show a small tooltip if the label is otherwise hidden.
- Use a spring-like transition that feels responsive without being distracting.
- Give the selected item a persistent active indicator.
- Return the icon to its normal state when the cursor leaves.
- Add a subtle click animation when an item is selected.
- Make keyboard focus visible for accessibility.
- On touchscreens, use tap feedback instead of hover behavior.

Use restrained electric-violet and cyan accents. Do not make every icon glow constantly.

If actual device haptics are supported on a mobile device, a very light vibration may be used where appropriate. Never pretend that vibration occurred on desktop when the device does not support it.

Hovering over an item must NOT automatically open a window. Hovering previews the interaction; clicking opens or switches to the feature.

Clicking a dock item should open its corresponding window, bring an existing window to the foreground, or restore it if minimized. Clicking the active item again should not create duplicate windows.

The dock must remain accessible even when all application windows are closed.

