# 11. SETTINGS — COMPLETE AND ACTUALLY USEFUL

Settings must be a real settings window with clearly grouped sections, not a page containing only a theme toggle.

Organize settings into simple categories. Use a clean list of sections with a detail panel, or another compact layout that fits the desktop.

## A. Appearance

Include:
- Dark theme.
- Light theme.
- System theme, if supported.
- Accent color selection.
- Reduced motion.
- Background animation on/off.
- Background animation intensity.
- Window transparency, if supported.
- Dock label visibility.
- Interface density, if supported.

Both light and dark themes must be fully designed. Do not merely invert the colors of the dark theme.

Changing a setting should update the interface immediately where appropriate and persist the selection.

## B. Desktop and windows

Include:
- Restore default window layout.
- Remember window positions.
- Default window size.
- Dock behavior.
- Animation preferences.
- Option to reopen the previous workspace, if supported.

Provide confirmation before resetting a custom layout.

## C. Notifications

Include:
- Task reminders.
- Upcoming deadline reminders.
- Focus-session completion alerts.
- Daily planning reminder.
- Weekly review reminder.
- Reminder time.
- Quiet hours.
- Browser notification permission status, if browser notifications are implemented.

Never claim a notification is enabled if permission has not been granted.

## D. Planning preferences

Include:
- Start of week.
- Preferred calendar view.
- Default task duration.
- Default focus duration.
- Working days.
- Typical availability.
- Timezone.
- Preferred reminder timing.

Use the correct local timezone and make it clear when a change affects scheduling.

## E. Focus preferences

Include:
- Default focus duration.
- Optional automatic break suggestions.
- Optional session completion sound.
- Default ambient sound.
- Default ambient volume.
- Full-screen focus option, if supported.

Do not enable sounds or notifications without the user's choice.

## F. AI Coach preferences

Include:
- Preferred response style: concise, balanced, or detailed.
- Coaching style: supportive, direct, or reflective.
- Whether the Coach should offer unsolicited suggestions, if implemented.
- Clear controls for saved Coach conversations.
- An explanation of what personal data the Coach can use.

Changing the response style must affect actual model instructions. It must not be a cosmetic setting.

## G. Privacy and data

Include:
- A plain-English explanation of how personal data is stored.
- Information about the anonymous account or session.
- Export personal data.
- Clear Coach conversation history.
- Clear focus history.
- Reset personal progress.
- Delete account and associated personal data.

Require confirmation for destructive actions. Explain the consequences before proceeding.

Export should produce a usable file containing the user's supported data.

Deleting data must actually delete it from the relevant storage systems, subject to the application's documented retention requirements. Do not claim deletion succeeded until the operation succeeds.

If the application uses anonymous authentication, explain that the display name is not a secure recovery method. Do not promise that users can recover their data on another device using only their name.

## H. Accessibility

Include:
- Reduced motion.
- Clear keyboard focus.
- Readable contrast.
- Text scaling, if supported.
- Keyboard navigation support.

## I. About IRL.OS

Include:
- Application name and version, if available.
- A short explanation of IRL.OS.
- Privacy information.
- Terms or support links only when real destinations exist.

## J. Save behavior

Prefer immediate saving with clear feedback for simple preferences.

For grouped forms, provide an explicit Save button when necessary.

Show success and error feedback. Never display a false “Saved” message when persistence fails.

